{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788972762,"nonce":"ZEPzR4a2RhRbpubHkXqQ6OkADIs=","title":"pawns {filter:\"hostile\"} misses manhunter animals that threats.hostiles_active counts","message":"The two hostile reads disagree on a manhunter animal, and the documented explanation for their disagreement does not apply.\n\n## Observed, openrun-20260909 day 4\n\nA \"Mad hare\" letter arrived. The screen:\n\n    threats: {danger: \"None\", hostiles: 1, hostiles_unpardoned: 1,\n              hostiles_active: 1, hostiles_dormant: 0, kinds: [\"hare x1\"]}\n\nAt the same tick:\n\n    pawns {filter:\"hostile\"} -\u003e list: [], total: 0\n      by_class: {wildlife: 56, colonist: 3, animal: 1}\n      skipped: {fogged_or_unspawned: 0, dead: 0}\n\n**`skipped.fogged_or_unspawned` is 0**, so this is not the known fog asymmetry that `checklists/turn.md` documents under `hostiles-standing` (\"a standing hostile you cannot see is real and unreachable, not a contradiction\", cc8988c miss 1). Nothing was skipped. The hare is in the `wildlife` bucket.\n\n## Likely cause\n\nA manhunting wild animal has **no faction**. A hostility filter keyed on `Faction`/`HostileTo(Faction.OfPlayer)` will not match it, while `ThreatSection` evidently counts it — correctly, since a manhunter pack is the thing that destroyed two turrets and an autocannon on the run that motivated the `destroyed` event. The right test for an animal is its `MentalStateDef` (`Manhunter` / `ManhunterPermanent`), not its faction.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n`turn.md`'s `hostiles-standing` trip-wire tells the agent to flag on `threats.hostiles_unpardoned` and to treat `pawns {filter:\"hostile\"}` as the fog-filtered twin. On this bench the twin reads **zero during an active manhunter event**. An agent that checks \"is anything actually attacking us\" with `pawns` gets \"no\" while something is hunting it, which is the same failure shape as the `hostiles` vs `danger` mismatch that made 31 digests wrong last run.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- With a manhunter animal on the map, `pawns {filter:\"hostile\"}` lists it, and its row says why it is hostile (the mental state), not merely that it is wildlife.\n- `threats.kinds` and the `pawns` hostile list agree on count in that state.\n- A non-manhunting wild animal is still NOT listed as hostile.\n- If the two are deliberately different sets, say so in `pawns`' own basis line and in `turn.md`'s `hostiles-standing` entry, naming the manhunter case — the current text explains only the fog difference and a reader will apply it to this.","files":null}]}